Phase 2: Data Understanding & Exploratory Analysis
Objective

The goal of Phase 2 is to develop a deep understanding of the dataset structure, semantics, and linguistic patterns in order to inform downstream NLP modeling decisions for the Fashion Copy & Attribute Studio project.

This phase focuses on:

Understanding catalog metadata

Identifying meaningful text fields

Analyzing linguistic structure of fashion descriptions

Justifying the choice of NLP techniques used in later phases

Dataset Overview

The project uses the Farfetch Listings Dataset, containing fashion product listings with both textual and structured metadata.

Key characteristics:

~188K fashion products

Focus on women’s and unisex fashion

Includes apparel, footwear, bags, accessories, and eyewear

Rich short product descriptions suitable for NLP tasks

Key Columns and Their Roles
Core NLP Columns
Column	Description	Usage
shortDescription	Short product description (e.g., “floral midi dress”)	Primary NLP input
brand.name	Brand identifier (e.g., Gucci, Prada)	Conditioning context
gender	Catalog category (women / unisex)	Contextual metadata
Supporting Metadata
Column	Purpose
priceInfo.finalPrice	Business context / demo filtering
priceInfo.isOnSale	Promotional context
stockTotal	Inventory signal
Ignored for Modeling (but retained)

Image URLs (images.*)

Structured size data (availableSizes)

Internal IDs (brand.id, merchantId)

Index columns (Unnamed: 0)

Data Quality Checks

Missing values: Expected in optional fields (e.g., sizes, discounts)

Duplicate rows: None detected

Brand names: Clean and normalized

Gender values: Limited to women and unisex by dataset scope

The dataset is clean and suitable for NLP modeling without aggressive preprocessing.

Brand Distribution Analysis

Top brands by frequency include:

Chanel (Pre-Owned)

Prada

Gucci

Saint Laurent

Dolce & Gabbana

Fendi

Valentino

This reflects realistic luxury fashion catalog distributions and ensures brand diversity for modeling.

Gender Distribution & Brand Interaction

Women: ~94%

Unisex: ~6%

Key findings:

Unisex items are primarily associated with accessories (bags, eyewear, sneakers)

Apparel-focused luxury brands are overwhelmingly women-categorized

Some brands are 100% unisex, reflecting intentional gender-neutral branding

Conclusion:
Gender categorization is product-type driven, not brand-driven.
Gender is treated as contextual metadata, not a prediction target.

Linguistic Analysis of Product Descriptions
Unigram Analysis

Most frequent tokens include:

dress, bag, print, logo, trousers, skirt, top

These tokens indicate product category and high-level attributes, but are insufficient alone.

Bigram (Phrase-Level) Analysis

Most frequent bigrams include:

shoulder bag

tote bag

crossbody bag

midi dress

mini dress

floral print

logo print

ankle boots

Key insight:
Fashion meaning is encoded in multi-word attribute phrases, not individual words.

Implications for NLP Modeling

Findings from Phase 2 directly inform modeling choices:

Attribute information is phrase-based

Sequence-level modeling is required

Simple keyword matching is insufficient

Therefore, downstream phases will use:

Named Entity Recognition (NER) for attribute extraction

Phrase-aware labeling (BIO format)

Controlled text generation conditioned on extracted attributes

Phase 2 Summary

Phase 2 establishes that the dataset:

Is clean, realistic, and industry-relevant

Contains attribute-dense short descriptions

Encodes fashion meaning through multi-token phrases

Supports sequence labeling and controlled generation tasks

This analysis provides a strong foundation for attribute schema design and NER modeling in Phase 3.
